#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147284 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "fb408a64-6d03-594d-92bd-b95c2a5c95a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5917" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+4z=0$ and $2x+4y+2z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 0, a_2, a_3\\rangle + t \\mathbf b, \\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nCalculate $a_2+a_3$, and find a possible direction vector $\\mathbf b$." :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0$. Substituting this into the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+2y+4z=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=-2z\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ and $y=-2z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 4y + 2z + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n2(0) + 4(-2z) + 2z + 18 &= 0 \\\\\n- 6z &= -18 \\\\\nz &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2z=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,-6,3\\rangle\n$$\n\nis a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+4z=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 1,2,4 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $2x+4y+2z+18=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 2,4,2 \\rangle$.\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{1} \\cdot n_{2} &= ⟨1, 2, 4⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 4, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}4 \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid 4 \\mid 2 \\\\\n&=-12i + 6j \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 12, 6, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} =\\dfrac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2) = \\langle -2, 1, 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + tb \\\\\nr(t) &= ⟨0,-6, 3⟩ + t⟨ - 2, 1, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\na_2 +a_3 = -6+3 = -3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b = \\langle -2,1,0\\rangle\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147285 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "68b206e1-07ee-5591-ac08-0109cb2063cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5920" :question/problem "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the following two planes:\n${2x + 4y + 5 = 0; 3y - 2z - 7 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+4y +5= 0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,4,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $3y-2z-7= 0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle 0,3,-2 \\rangle$.\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= n_{1} \\cdot n_{2} \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 4, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 3,-2⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}0 \\\\\n&= 0 \\mid 3 \\mid - 2 \\\\\n&=-8i + 4j + 6k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 8, 4, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147286 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/id #uuid "ae2be3b1-50fb-5abe-9f18-002a88f1de61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5921" :question/problem "Given the two planes $4x-2y=0$ and $-2x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $\\mathbf{0}$.\n2. The planes are parallel but not coincident.\n3. The planes intersect on a straight line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $4x-2y=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 4,-2,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $-2x+y+1=0$, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -2,1,0 \\rangle$.\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n$n_{1} \\cdot n_{2}|= ⟨4,-2, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 2, 1, 0⟩; n|= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}0; - 2 \\mid 1 \\mid 0; = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\mathbf{n}_1 \\times \\mathbf{n}_2 =\\mathbf{0}$, the vectors $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2$ are parallel. However, the first plane passes through the origin $O(0,0,0)$ but the second plane doesn't: $-2(0)+(0)+1=1 \\neq 0$ So, the planes are parallel but not coincident.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the planes are parallel but not coincident, they cannot intersect each other.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "939e9973-cb8d-54aa-a7e6-965adb9bf94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146876} {:answer/id #uuid "b091c44e-c006-5b37-85b6-190cdc188a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146877} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fe2a3a-8c62-5389-a161-a25f6e8a33cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146878} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2bcc97-cf0e-5657-8010-ae978b880459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146879} {:answer/id #uuid "68a96f68-b656-59fb-9345-0525fd9ebd33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "939e9973-cb8d-54aa-a7e6-965adb9bf94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146876} :answer-field/id #uuid "57427c13-e607-5012-b842-cb76e784fdd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146875}] :question/id #uuid "ddee53f3-5b0d-5dc0-9b9e-825db292f3e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71110" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x - y + z = 0$ and $x - z + 4 = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0$.\n2. The planes are parallel.\n3. The planes intersect on a line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98dad773-e343-5720-b6d0-06acff1fcc18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-1, b = ⟨1, 0, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146894} {:answer/id #uuid "2df7b237-0cf7-5db4-a613-d9911a551947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-1, b = ⟨ - 1, 1, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146895} {:answer/id #uuid "37ff0265-8d39-5cfc-b9a1-4b42b3ef6549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-5, b = ⟨1, 0, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146896} {:answer/id #uuid "4b250100-69cb-525f-8e37-ceb2e6d50081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-2, b = ⟨ - 1, 1, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146897} {:answer/id #uuid "669966ee-f3cb-5ad2-81fa-197cc11629e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-2, b = ⟨1, 0, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b250100-69cb-525f-8e37-ceb2e6d50081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a_{1} + a_{2} =-2, b = ⟨ - 1, 1, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146897} :answer-field/id #uuid "50693ecf-036a-5c40-a007-20ea1b1a1626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146893}] :question/id #uuid "c01a5d8f-f2ce-5a5d-bfd0-e648dd7fcc1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71187" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $-x + y - 2z = 0$ and $-x - 2y + z - 3 = 0$ is given by the line\n\n$r(t) = ⟨a_{1}, a_{2}, 0⟩ + tb, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$.\n\nCalculate $a_{1} + a_{2}$, and find a possible direction vector $b$."}]}
